Texas Republican congressman Mac  Thornberry is chair of the House Armed  Services Committee and he joins us  tonight from Capitol Hill mr. chairman I  want to pick up on the comment from  defense secretary mattis that he wants  to see international chemical weapons  inspectors come into Syria this weekend  does that indicate to you that there  will be no u.s. strike until those  inspectors are safely out of Syria well  I think it's important that we not say  when or how or where we may act clearly  if Assad allows these chemical  inspectors in we will be very cautious  about anything that could endanger them  but at the same time I don't think that  most people believe there's a high  likelihood is gonna let actual  inspectors in to prove what he has done  which is a terrible atrocity we can't  lose sight of that as we have mentioned  president Trump at least in his tweets  has been sending mixed messages  I forget about the time and I understand  your sensitivity about that are you  persuaded that he is eventually going to  order a military strike to deter or at  least under message of Bashar Assad I  know you convinced that's the right  answer well I'm convinced he has not yet  reached a decision on a military element  I believe they are looking at diplomatic  economic as well as military options you  know I do remember what happened a year  ago where there was a chemical attack  that killed close to a hundred people  there was a targeted strike against  those Syrian military units that had  used chemical weapons and in the process  about 17% of the Syrian Air Force was  destroyed I think while it did not stop  them from using chemical weapons it  slowed them down a little bit and so  part of what the international community  needs to think about is how to send a  message not go so far as to escalate but  also not do nothing which would I think  send the message that this is okay well  you say not ask  but there are a number of your  colleagues in Congress who are  suggesting if we're gonna take another  military strike that the US should  escalate in other words not the same  kind of attack as last time but  something more that indicates that in a  sense the Western community is upping  the ante if Bashar al-assad continues  with this kind of behavior do you agree  with that well I agree that that he  needs to pay a price  but I also believe the United States is  not going to fix all of the problems in  Syria and we do not want to get involved  in the Syrian civil war what we want to  do with our international partners is  say that it send a clear message if with  force if necessary and I would support  such an action that that this is beyond  the realm of human what humanity can  tolerate to kill innocent people with  these horrible weapons that were  outlawed nearly a hundred years ago  and and so I think we need to send a  clear message but obviously none of us  want to get into some sort of escalation  with Russia and others well I was going  to pick up exactly on that point which  is that the Russians have been talking  tough and yesterday they said that if we  did launch a military strike they would  not only try to shoot down our missiles  but might also respond to the ships the  US ships that have fired them do you  worry that this could turn into a  confrontation between the US and Russia  well I think it has to be carefully  calibrated and that's why I mentioned  the specific goal of last year of  military strikes against the Syrian  units that use these chemical weapons to  kill innocent people  carefully targeted not going after those  other installations where Russians may  be present but but being focused but at  the same time sending a clear message  and I think that's the kind of  appropriate response proportional  response that probably continues to make  sense  finally chairman last week the president  said that he wants to get out of Syria  soon and in fact he reportedly had to be  persuaded that he would keep us force  on the ground in Syria for six months  now he may be about to launch a military  strike against Syria which has raised  the question for a lot of people in fact  do we have a coherent strategy with how  to deal with the situation in Syria well  I think it's important to keep in mind  number one we're in Syria to fight Isis  and we have had tremendous success in  narrowing down the area that Isis  controls we have not eradicated them  however and what we want to make sure is  that we have finished the job  so that Isis 3.0 cannot reappear  remember the terrible mistake President  Obama made when he completely got out of  Iraq and Isis then came back and and we  had to go in with much higher costs in  all respects in order to get rid of them  we are not in Syria to fix all of the  political mess the civil war that  President Trump inherited but you have  this chemical weapons issue which again  as secretary mattis testified today is  really an issue for civilization that we  can't just turn away from so we need to  keep our interests in mind its terrorism  but also acknowledge that we have these  other targeted interests without trying  to go in and fix everything that's  broken there Chairman Mac Thornberry of  the House Armed Services Committee  chairman thanks so much for joining us  tonight thank you sir
 

